# The prebuilt object is compared byte-for-byte against a fresh compile by
# kernel/build.rs, so any line-ending translation would both fail that check and
# corrupt the object actually linked into the kernel.  Git would almost certainly
# infer "binary" from the NUL bytes in an ELF file and leave it alone -- but that
# is an inference, and it is the sort that holds until the day it doesn't.  On a
# checkout with core.autocrlf=true (this tree happens to use `input`, which only
# converts on commit; other lanes' machines need not) a misdetection would
# rewrite bytes in an artifact whose whole purpose is to be byte-exact.  Say it
# explicitly instead of relying on the heuristic.
*.o -text -diff

# stamp.txt is read with .trim() on both the Rust and Python sides, so its line
# endings do not affect the comparison -- pin it to LF anyway so the file is
# identical everywhere and a diff never shows a whole-file change.
stamp.txt text eol=lf
